.TH ODM 1 "2026-07-21" "odm" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
odm \- Oryn Download Manager, an aria2c-inspired CLI download manager with a Connection Balancer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B odm
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI <URL>
.RI [ URL ... ]
.br
.B odm
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RB " \fI" URL1,URL2,... "\fR"
.br
.B odm
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.B \-i
.I file-list.txt
.br
.B odm
.B \-\-rpc
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B odm
is a CLI download manager written in Go and inspired by
.BR aria2c (1).
It ships as a single static binary with no runtime dependencies.
.PP
Its core differentiator is the
.BR "Connection Balancer" :
automatic allocation of parallel connections that adapts between
single-file and many-file (batch) modes, so that one connection budget
.RB ( \-c )
is split sensibly across files instead of the user computing
connections-per-file by hand.
.PP
It also has a pacman / CachyOS-style
.RB ( ILoveCandy )
progress bar and a JSON-RPC 2.0 + WebSocket RPC server so other programs
(CLIs, GUIs, scripts) can drive it \(em the same relationship
.BR aria2c (1)
has with AriaNg.
.SH QUICK START
The three canonical invocations:
.PP
1. Single file, 16 parallel connections to one server for one file:
.PP
.RS
.B odm \-c 16 https://files.test.xyz/file.tar.gz
.RE
.PP
2. Batch (no \fB\-sf\fR): 16 URLs, 1 connection per file, 16 files run in
parallel.
.B RECOMMENDED:
space-separated positional args (comma-safe).
.PP
.RS
.B odm \-c 16 https://files.test.xyz/file1.tar.gz url2 url3 ...   # 16 urls
.RE
.PP
3. Batch with \fB\-sf 4\fR: 16 connections split 4 per file \(-> 4 files run
in parallel (the rest queue automatically as slots free).
.PP
.RS
.B odm \-c 16 \-sf 4 https://files.test.xyz/file1.tar.gz url2 url3 ...
.RE
.PP
For large batches (>10 URLs) prefer an input file:
.PP
.RS
.B odm \-i file-list.txt        # one URL per line, '#' comments and blanks skipped
.RE
.SH "CONNECTION BALANCER"
.I ( \-c , \-sf )
.PP
.B \-c
is the
.I total
parallel-connection budget. What it controls depends on the mode:
.TS
allbox tab(@);
cb cb cb cb
l lw(2.5i) lw(3i).
Mode@Condition@What \fB\-c\fR controls@per-file connections
A@one URL (\fB\-sf\fR ignored)@whole budget \(-> the one file@\fBmin(\-c, \-\-max\-connections)\fR
B@many URLs, no \fB\-sf\fR@how many files run in parallel@1 each
C@many URLs + \fB\-sf N\fR@\fBfloor(\-c / N)\fR files run in parallel@N (remainder distributed to the first files)
.TE
.PP
.BR \-\-max\-connections
(default
.BR 32 )
is a soft ceiling: going above it just prints a warning
.RB ( "connections above 32 may get throttled/blocked by some servers" ),
since many CDN/servers treat >~30 concurrent connections from one IP as
abusive. Power users on a LAN / local CDN can raise it.
.PP
Files that don't support HTTP range requests (the probe falls back through
HEAD \(-> ranged GET \(-> single-stream) are automatically given a single
connection, and the freed budget is redistributed to the other files in the
same scheduling pass. (Mid-flight dynamic rebalancing of
.I already-downloading
chunks is a roadmap item, not in this release.)
.SS "Why a chunk queue (work-stealing)"
Each file is split into small chunks (default
.BR \-\-chunk\-size 4M )
pushed into a shared per-file queue. Worker goroutines \(em one per allocated
connection \(em pull the next chunk as soon as they finish the previous one.
This avoids the classic
.I straggler problem
of a static equal-split: a slow connection just processes fewer chunks
instead of holding the whole file back.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Connection budget (the Balancer auto-splits \fB\-c\fR across files)"
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-connections " \fIN\fR"
total parallel-connection budget
.RI ( default " 5" )
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-connections " \fIN\fR"
ceiling; exceeding it just warns
.RI ( default " 32" )
.TP
.BR \-\-split-file ", " \-sf " \fIN\fR"
connections per file in batch mode
.RI ( unset " = 1 per file" )
.SS "Inputs / output"
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fINAME\fR"
output filename (single-file only)
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir " \fIPATH\fR"
destination directory
.RI ( default " cwd" )
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-input-file " \fIFILE\fR"
read URL list from FILE (Metalink4
.IR .meta4 /.metalink
supported)
.SS "Behavior"
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
skip the confirmation prompt
.TP
.BR \-\-dry\-run
probe every URL and show the download plan; nothing is downloaded
.TP
.BR -q ", " \-\-quiet
no progress bar (cron/scripts); also skips the prompt
.TP
.BR \-\-auto\-rename
existing destination is kept; save as name.<N>.ext instead (mutually exclusive with --skip-existing)
.TP
.BR \-\-skip\-existing
skip files already present with a matching size
.TP
.BR \-\-session\-log " \fIFILE\fR"
append JSONL progress/summary events to FILE (for wrappers/GUIs)
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-continue
resume an incomplete file via the
.B .odm
control file
.RI ( default " on" )
.TP
.BR -s ", " \-\-chunk-size " \fISIZE\fR"
work-stealing chunk size, e.g.
.B 4M
.RI ( default " 4M" )
.SS "HTTP / network"
.TP
.BR -n ", " \-\-max\-redirect " \fIN\fR"
redirect hops to follow
.RI ( default " 5" )
.TP
.BR -r ", " \-\-retry " \fIN\fR"
retries per segment on failure
.RI ( default " 3" )
.TP
.BR -w ", " \-\-retry\-wait " \fISEC\fR"
delay between retries
.RI ( default " 2" )
.TP
.BR -t ", " \-\-timeout " \fISEC\fR"
dial+headers timeout
.RI ( default " 30" )
.TP
.BR -u ", " \-\-user\-agent " \fIUA\fR"
custom User-Agent
.RI ( default " odm/<version>" )
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-header " \fIK:V\fR"
add a custom header (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-\-load\-cookies " \fIFILE\fR"
load a Netscape cookies.txt and send it as a Cookie header
.TP
.BR \-\-referer " \fIURL\fR"
set the Referer header
.TP
.BR -p ", " \-\-proxy " \fIURL\fR"
http/https/socks5 proxy
.TP
.BR \-\-check\-certificate " \fIBOOL\fR"
verify TLS
.RI ( default " true" )
.TP
.BR \-\-checksum " \fIALGO:HASH\fR"
verify md5/sha1/sha256
.TP
.BR \-\-checksum\-url " \fIURL\fR"
fetch the checksum from a sidecar URL (sha256sum/md5sum-style file or bare hash)
.TP
.BR \-\-mirror " \fIURL\fR"
alternate URL serving the same file; chunk requests rotate across all sources (repeatable)
.TP
.BR -l ", " \-\-limit\-rate " \fIRATE\fR"
global speed limit, e.g.
.BR 5M / 500K
.TP
.BR \-\-limit\-rate\-per\-task " \fIRATE\fR"
per-task speed cap (stacked on global), e.g.
.BR 2M
.SS "Config / logging / RPC"
.TP
.BR \-\-config " \fIPATH\fR"
config file path
.RI ( default " /etc/odm/config.conf" )
.TP
.BR -L ", " \-\-log " \fIFILE\fR"
mirror logs to FILE
.TP
.BR \-\-log\-level " \fILEVEL\fR"
debug|info|warn|error
.RI ( default " info" )
.TP
.BR \-\-rpc
run as the RPC server (daemon)
.TP
.BR \-\-rpc\-listen\-port " \fIPORT\fR"
.RI ( default " 6900" )
.TP
.BR \-\-rpc\-listen\-all
bind 0.0.0.0 (default 127.0.0.1)
.TP
.BR \-\-rpc\-secret " \fITOKEN\fR"
auth for RPC (token:<secret> param or ?secret=)
.TP
.BR \-\-rpc\-tls\-cert " \fIFILE\fR"
TLS certificate path (PEM); enables HTTPS
.TP
.BR \-\-rpc\-tls\-key " \fIFILE\fR"
TLS private key path (PEM); requires \fB\-\-rpc\-tls\-cert\fR
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
print version and exit
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
show help
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
Config source priority (a defaulted flag never overwrites a value the user put
in a file):
.PP
.RS
CLI args > ~/.config/odm/config.conf > /etc/odm/config.conf > defaults
.RE
.PP
The file is
.BR "key = value" ,
one per line,
.B #
for comments; key names match the CLI long-flags (without
.BR \-\- ).
See
.I /etc/odm/config.conf.example
for a fully-commented template.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
connections        = 5
max-connections    = 32
split-file         =
dir                 = /home/user/Downloads
max-redirect        = 5
retry               = 3
retry-wait          = 2
timeout             = 30
user-agent          = odm/1.0
load-cookies        =
check-certificate   = true
profile             = odm
continue            = true
quiet               = false
dry-run             = false
auto-rename         = false
skip-existing       = false
limit-rate          =
limit-rate-per-task  =
# RPC
rpc                 = false
rpc-listen-port     = 6900
rpc-listen-all       = false
rpc-secret          =
rpc-tls-cert        =
rpc-tls-key         =
# Logging
log                 =
log-level           = info
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Comma note:
the legacy
.RB \(dq url1,url2,... \(dq
single-argument form is still supported, but a comma
.I inside
a URL (e.g.
.BR ?ids=1,2,3 )
is ambiguous, so
.B space-separated positional args
are the recommended form. URLs with a literal comma must use either
space-separated form or
.BR \-i .
.SH "RATE LIMITING"
.BR \-\-limit\-rate
is enforced with a
.I global token bucket
shared across
.I all
active workers of
.I all
tasks, rather than splitting the limit per connection. Throttling happens at
the data-stream level (bytes read from the network, before writing to disk),
so the aggregate throughput stays close to the configured cap regardless of
how many connections are alive or how the batch queue advances.
.PP
An optional
.BR \-\-limit\-rate\-per\-task
adds a
.I per-task token bucket
stacked on top of the global one. Each worker acquires tokens from both
buckets before writing to disk, so a single task is capped to
.BR min ( per-task, global )
and the sum across all tasks never exceeds the global cap. Both limits can
be changed at runtime via RPC
.BR odm.changeOption .
.SH RESUME
With
.B \-x
/
.B \-\-continue
(on by default), each download writes a
.I <file>.odm
control file recording which chunks are already done (JSON: URL, total size,
chunk size, and the list of completed chunks). On re-run with the same
destination present, only the un-finished chunks are re-fetched \(em no
corruption, no restart. The control file is deleted on a clean completion.
.SH "PROGRESS BAR"
When stderr/stdout is a TTY, odm renders a pacman / CachyOS-style
.RB ( ILoveCandy )
progress bar. The per-file line format is:
.PP
.RS
\fI<file_name>\fR   \fI<done/total>\fR   \fI<speed>\fR/s   \fI<ETA>\fR   [\fIx<N>\fR-\fI<progress-bar>\fR]   \fI<percent>\fR%
.RE
.PP
where
.B <done/total>
is the hybrid downloaded/total size with compact suffixes (e.g.
.BR 42.0M/256.0M )
that grows live as bytes arrive,
.B x<N>
is the number of parallel connections currently used by that file (shown inside
the bar brackets as a prefix, e.g.
.BR [x4-----c  o  o  o  o] ),
.B c
is the pacman icon moving left to right as progress advances,
.B o
is a dot not yet eaten, and blank/-
is already eaten (at 100% the whole bar is blank/dashes).
.PP
Color coding (when the terminal supports ANSI colors; disabled automatically
on non-TTY or
.BR NO_COLOR ):
completed = green, downloading = yellow, retrying after an error = red,
queued/waiting = dim/grey.
.PP
If stdout is not a terminal
.RI ( \-\-quiet
or redirected to a file/pipe) the progress bar is replaced with periodic log
lines without ANSI cursor control.
.SH "RPC SERVER"
Run odm as a daemon exposing JSON-RPC 2.0 + WebSocket so third-party
GUIs/scripts can be built on top without parsing terminal output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
POST  http://127.0.0.1:6900/rpc   (JSON-RPC 2.0)
 WS   ws://127.0.0.1:6900/ws      (event notifications)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Default bind is
.B 127.0.0.1
(safe).
.B \-\-rpc\-listen\-all
binds
.B 0.0.0.0
\(em pair that with
.BR \-\-rpc\-secret .
.PP
TLS encryption can be enabled with
.B \-\-rpc\-tls\-cert
and
.BR \-\-rpc\-tls\-key ;
when both are provided the server switches to HTTPS/WSS and the listener
prints a
.B https://
URL. Without TLS, a
.B http://
URL is shown (the default).
.PP
Auth is aria2-style: the first JSON-RPC param is
.RB \(dq token:<secret> \(dq
(or
.B ?secret=<value>
on the WebSocket upgrade).
.SS "Quickstart with curl"
.PP
.RS
.nf
odm \-\-rpc \-\-rpc\-secret hunter2 \-\-rpc\-listen\-port 6900 \-d ~/Downloads &

# add a download; note the token:<secret> first param
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:6900/rpc \e
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \e
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"odm.addUri","params":["token:hunter2","https://files.test.xyz/x.tar.gz"],"id":1}'

# ask its status
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:6900/rpc \e
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \e
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"odm.tellStatus","params":["token:hunter2","odm-001"],"id":2}'
.fi
.RE
.SS Methods
.TS
allbox tab(@);
lw(3i) lw(3.3i).
Method@Description
odm.addUri@Add a single URL to the queue.
odm.addBatch@Add many URLs at once.
odm.pause / odm.pauseAll@Pause a task / all tasks.
odm.unpause / odm.unpauseAll@Resume paused task(s).
odm.remove@Cancel and remove a task.
odm.tellStatus@Detailed status of one task (progress, speed, conns, ETA).
odm.tellActive / odm.tellWaiting / odm.tellStopped@List active / queued / finished tasks.
odm.changeOption@Change options at runtime: max-download-limit, max-download-limit-per-task, connections.
odm.getGlobalStat@Global stats (active/waiting/stopped counts).
odm.getVersion@Version + enabled features.
odm.shutdown@Shut the daemon down.
.TE
.SS "Events (WebSocket /ws)"
All five download events are emitted over the
.B /ws
fan-out:
.TS
allbox tab(@);
l l.
Event@When
onDownloadStart@a task is added via odm.addUri
onDownloadProgress@periodic snapshot \(em throttled to ~250ms
onDownloadComplete@a task finishes cleanly
onDownloadError@a task fails / is cancelled
onDownloadPause@a task is paused via odm.pause
.TE
.PP
Each event's
.B params
carries the same field set as
.BR odm.tellStatus 's
result.
.SH "EXIT CODES"
.TS
allbox tab(@);
l l.
Code@Meaning
0@All downloads succeeded
1@General error / invalid argument
2@Network error (all retries exhausted)
3@Partial failure in a batch (some files failed)
4@Cancelled by the user
.TE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, disables colored progress output even when writing to a TTY.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/odm/config.conf
System-wide default config (root-owned,
.BR 0644 ,
per the security guidance).
.TP
.I ~/.config/odm/config.conf
Per-user overrides.
.TP
.I configs/odm.conf.example
Fully-commented template shipped with the source.
.TP
.I <file>.odm
Per-task resume control file, deleted on a clean completion.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Download a single file with 16 connections into the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.B odm \-c 16 https://files.test.xyz/file.tar.gz
.RE
.PP
Batch download a space-separated list, 5 files at a time (the default
.BR \-c 5 ),
1 connection each:
.PP
.RS
.B odm url1 url2 url3 url4 url5 url6
.RE
.PP
Batch with explicit split: 8 connections split 4 per file \-> 2 files at a
time, resuming any incomplete ones, dumping progress to logs only:
.PP
.RS
.B odm \-c 8 \-sf 4 \-x \-q \-i list.txt
.RE
.PP
Throttle to 5 MiB/s aggregate and verify a sha256 after completion:
.PP
.RS
.B odm \-\-limit\-rate 5M \-\-checksum sha256:9f86... https://files.test.xyz/x.iso
.RE
.PP
Run the RPC daemon on port 6900 with a shared secret, downloads going to
~/Downloads:
.PP
.RS
.B odm \-\-rpc \-\-rpc\-secret hunter2 \-\-rpc\-listen\-port 6900 \-d ~/Downloads
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR aria2c (1),
.BR curl (1)
.SH BUGS
.LP
Mid-flight connection reallocation (\fBchangeOption\fR with key
\fBconnections\fR) is partially supported: graceful drain (excess workers
finish their current chunk and retire) is implemented; dynamic rebalancing
of already-dispatched chunks across tasks is a roadmap item.
.LP
HTTP/2 / HTTP/3 multiplexing is deliberately not a core feature: odm's
value proposition is opening many independent TCP connections; against
HTTP/2-only servers it falls back to single-stream.
.SH AUTHOR
.LP
The odm project \(em see the source repository.
